      "problem": "Each of the three shapes in the model below represents one whole.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129499/assets/222af9ec253a6624ece646225db926069cd7be3ac416fe846a9216360981f8cb.png)\n\n \n\nFrom left to right, what numbers are missing from the division problem below?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129499/assets/07ee75200e94915edcb2c923fed34e49749dbc449e95358f9aebec9c4f5ea83e.svg)",
      "worked_solution": "In the model, there are $\\color{red}3$ shapes. Each shape consists of $4$ pieces that each represent $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a whole. There are $\\color{blue}12$ pieces in total.\n\nThe model tells us that when we divide $\\color{red}3$ wholes into quarters, we will be left with $\\color{blue}12$ pieces.\n\nTherefore, the division problem is:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14129499/assets/5eba82b4c27974b0bef093741513020db51d0923213d950f9723928825f6df75.svg)\n\nSo, the missing numbers are $\\color{red}3$ and $\\color{blue}12.$",
